For this model, use the OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions SDK at the model level:

```jsonc
"zai-glm-5-2": {
  "provider": {
    "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible"
  },
  // ...the rest of the model definition
}
```

This keeps the Mistral provider's API URL and credentials, still sends `model: "zai-glm-5-2"`, and uses the Chat Completions stream parser that supports incremental tool-call deltas. Keep this override inside the model definition; setting `npm` on the top-level Mistral provider would change the SDK for every Mistral model.

This alone didn't work, because I get a different ValidationError with the `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` provider. I had to create a custom plugin that modifies the messages to get it to work:

Here's how to use GLM-5.2 on Mistral:

1. Define an OpenAI provider in `opencode.jsonc` (or define the provider only for the individual model as the agent above has suggested):
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "mistral-openai": {
      "name": "Mistral (OpenAI)",
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "models": {
        "zai-glm-5-2": {
          "name": "GLM-5.2",
          "attachment": true,
          "reasoning": true,
          "temperature": true,
          "tool_call": true,
          "limit": {
            "context": 1000000,
            "output": 131072
          },
          "modalities": {
            "input": [
              "text"
            ],
            "output": [
              "text"
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://api.mistral.ai/v1"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

2. Connect the new provider using `/connect` and enter the Mistral API key
3. Copy the plugin file [mistral-glm-model.ts](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/31229323/mistral-glm-model.js) into directory `~/.config/opencode/plugins/` (note the file extension **.ts**), where it will be picked up automatically on the next restart of OpenCode without additional configuration
---
Anyone wanting to fix the bug in the OpenCode source can use the plugin's source to figure out what has to be filtered in the responses to get the model to work.
  1. `delta.content` may arrive as an array of structured parts
     (e.g. `[{type:"thinking", thinking:[{type:"text", text:"..."}], closed:true}]`).
     The SDK schema requires `content: z.string().nullish()`, so we flatten
     it: text parts → concatenated string for `content`, thinking parts →
     concatenated string for `reasoning_content`.
  2. Tool-call chunks may arrive without an `id` field on continuation
     fragments. The SDK schema marks `id` as `nullish` so missing ids pass
     validation, but the streaming tool-call tracker throws if the first
     delta carrying `function.name` has no id. We ensure every tool-call
     delta with a name carries a stable, unique id.

Additionally, the outgoing request body is rewritten so assistant reasoning
is sent back in Mistral's ThinkChunk content-array format (Mistral rejects
`reasoning_content` on incoming requests with HTTP 422).

The plugin injects a custom `fetch` only into providers that (a) have
`@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` as their npm package, (b) have a provider id
containing "mistral", and (c) define the `zai-glm-5-2` model.
